  ('3a37c1d4-9548-5a14-b02e-f9fe876e94a4', 'fc7a6f02-f417-5bcf-a8ad-4f891c9aa3b3', 1, true, 'Advantages with examples (max 3): faster / more accurate diagnosis in medicine; fewer accidents with self-driving cars; fraud detection; personalised education; dangerous / repetitive work done by robots; 24/7
Disadvantages with examples (max 3): job losses (drivers, cashiers); bias in hiring / loans; privacy / surveillance; deepfakes / misinformation; unclear accountability; black-box decisions
Conclusion (1) that weighs the points
[Max 6] — at least two areas must be referred to for full marks', '[{"criterion":"Advantage with an example","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second advantage in another area","marks":1},{"criterion":"Disadvantage with an example","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second disadvantage","marks":1},{"criterion":"Points developed / explained","marks":1},{"criterion":"Reasoned conclusion","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'In medicine, AI can analyse X-rays faster and sometimes more accurately than doctors, so diseases are found earlier. In transport, self-driving cars could reduce accidents caused by tired or distracted drivers, and AI can do dangerous jobs in mines or factories. However, AI may replace drivers, cashiers and call-centre staff, causing unemployment. AI trained on biased data can make unfair decisions about loans or jobs, and face recognition threatens privacy. It is also unclear who is responsible when an AI makes a harmful mistake. Overall, AI can do more good than harm only if it is regulated, trained on fair data and its decisions are checked by people.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('6c71919e-1f48-5130-842e-387497bed3ee', 'cfe688d3-2430-5d98-a015-5695a7ba25fe', 1, true, 'AI–R2, ML–R3, DL–R1. One mark per correct line.', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"pairs":{"L1":"R2","L2":"R3","L3":"R1"}}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('8c65012f-6592-53a7-893d-939543195746', '1ae33011-9c77-5bd0-886f-76958f5b0e7c', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): many-layered neural networks vs simpler models; much more data; much more computing power; features learnt automatically vs chosen by humans; harder to explain; DL is a subset of ML', '[{"criterion":"First valid difference","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second, different valid difference","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Deep learning uses neural networks with many hidden layers. 2 Deep learning needs much larger amounts of data and computing power.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('0c89d81c-19fd-57fa-bde5-59a52a9cf9c9', 'd9c61b73-50b6-5a2f-8275-e4fe6a6d6c2b', 1, true, 'Any three (1 each): trained on many photos labelled with the flower name; finds patterns / features (colour, shape of petals); builds a model / neural network that predicts the label of a new photo; errors compared with correct labels and weights adjusted; accuracy improves with more data / repeated training', '[{"criterion":"Labelled training photos","marks":1},{"criterion":"Finds patterns / builds a model","marks":1},{"criterion":"Adjusts using errors / improves with data","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The system is trained on thousands of photos, each labelled with the name of the flower. It finds patterns such as petal shape and colour and builds a model that predicts the name for a new photo. Its wrong predictions are used to adjust the model, so it becomes more accurate with more training data.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('a4b46d24-7341-58a6-b4c0-9d8248702f52', '85c24188-e78e-569b-92ff-48254ee9e2a8', 1, true, 'Medicine (1): disease diagnosis; medical image interpretation; drug discovery; personalised treatment
