  ('6e4ebee6-d2de-5ba9-8977-d1994603537b', 'e752b4f5-3342-5c8c-976f-e9f3b537d998', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): what vs how; execution order not clearly delineated vs clearly defined sequence of instructions; logic / query / functional vs procedural / object-oriented; little or no changing state vs variables changed by assignment', '[{"criterion":"First valid difference","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second, different valid difference","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Declarative languages describe what result is wanted, whereas imperative languages describe how to get it. 2 An imperative program is a clearly defined sequence of instructions, whereas in a declarative program the system decides the order of execution.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('b0de660a-0437-5e5f-9569-776b077e0e64', '4d72793d-1e9b-53dd-99de-f04062b7a240', 1, true, 'Tracker (max 2): low-level / assembly (1) — small memory, fast code, direct control of sensors / battery (1)
Phone app (max 2): high-level (1) — portable to Android and iPhone / faster development / easier to maintain / graphical interface libraries (1)', '[{"criterion":"Tracker: low-level","marks":1},{"criterion":"Tracker: justification","marks":1},{"criterion":"App: high-level","marks":1},{"criterion":"App: justification","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The tracker program should be written in a low-level language such as assembly, because it must fit into 64 KB and run efficiently on the small processor while controlling the sensors directly. The phone app should be written in a high-level language, because it can be developed faster, uses libraries for the user interface and can be translated to run on both Android and iPhone.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('2aa7ee83-316c-5725-8c04-9e266c8de683', '040297a1-3267-5313-8dfb-136147305f54', 1, true, 'Comparison points (max 4), each with both sides: readability / ease; portability; translation (compiler / interpreter vs assembler / none); hardware control; speed / memory; development time; one-to-many vs one-to-one
Examples (1): correct example of each (e.g. Python and assembly)
Situations (1): appropriate use of each (e.g. apps vs device drivers / embedded systems)', '[{"criterion":"First comparison point","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second comparison point","marks":1},{"criterion":"Third comparison point","marks":1},{"criterion":"Fourth comparison point","marks":1},{"criterion":"Example of each type","marks":1},{"criterion":"Situation for each type","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'High-level languages such as Python use English-like keywords, so they are easier to read, write and debug than low-level languages such as assembly, which use mnemonics. High-level programs are portable, whereas low-level programs are written for one processor. High-level code is translated by a compiler or interpreter, and each statement becomes many machine instructions; assembly is translated one-to-one by an assembler. Low-level languages give direct control of registers and memory and produce faster, smaller programs. High-level languages are used for applications and websites, while low-level languages are used for device drivers and embedded systems with little memory.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
