  ('6d57e610-bd63-5edc-894b-cc657c9b47a4', '1edd8f1f-ba49-5a7d-b596-c703b3121285', 1, true, '- Public: unique on the Internet, assigned by the ISP, reachable from anywhere (1)
- Private: used only inside a LAN (e.g. 192.168.x.x), reused in many networks, not reachable directly from the Internet (1)
- Security: private devices are hidden behind the router / NAT so outsiders cannot connect directly; public addresses can be attacked and need a firewall (1)', '[{"criterion":"Public","marks":1},{"criterion":"Private","marks":1},{"criterion":"Security","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'A public address is unique on the Internet and can be reached from anywhere; a private address such as 192.168.1.5 works only inside the LAN. Devices with private addresses are hidden behind the router''s NAT, so attackers cannot connect to them directly, while public addresses need firewall protection.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('81fa4274-7902-5ad8-9632-e99d16367e15', 'fda8e1ba-5686-5913-a465-a2c437c1533d', 1, true, 'True.', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"correct":true}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('3d3d41d4-3265-5fe1-a8ca-1af4b0a3ffbc', 'c0686900-acf5-57db-94ab-75258d3574ee', 1, true, '- Printer must always be found at the same address so computers can send print jobs to it (1)
- laptops come and go; DHCP gives them an address automatically and reuses addresses, no manual setup (1)', '[{"criterion":"Printer","marks":1},{"criterion":"Laptops","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Computers must always find the printer at the same address, so it is static; laptops join and leave, so DHCP gives them any free address automatically.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('9218ea83-20e7-5d98-9505-e6e995baa5d0', 'e8f1e8bb-d182-5dd0-8bd1-6c02f543ec3c', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each), both sides: physical vs logical; set by manufacturer vs network / DHCP; fixed vs can change; 48-bit hex vs 32-bit denary / 128-bit; used by switches in a LAN vs routers between networks', '[{"criterion":"1","marks":1},{"criterion":"2","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 A MAC address is a physical address set by the manufacturer, while an IP address is a logical address given by the network. 2 The MAC address is used within the LAN by switches, while the IP address is used by routers to send packets between networks.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('e41e1bb9-2c5f-5348-8c40-b0a4a409600a', '3f867f1d-fe01-574c-bd29-27b227d8540a', 1, true, 'B', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"correct":"B"}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('2af03d9f-b76e-5d09-83c4-60e75b5e8c2b', '5cdcea20-e72c-56f7-9300-9792815a0860', 1, true, '- Routers use IP addresses to bring the packet to the school network (1)
- the school router finds the laptop''s MAC address from its IP (ARP) (1)
- the switch uses the destination MAC address to send the frame only to the laptop''s port; MAC changes at every hop (1)', '[{"criterion":"IP to network","marks":1},{"criterion":"ARP","marks":1},{"criterion":"Switch delivers by MAC","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Routers forward the packet using the IP address until it reaches the school router. The router uses ARP to find the MAC address that belongs to the laptop''s IP, and the switch then uses that MAC address to send the frame only to the laptop.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('9853186b-94ed-5926-b026-bd9d797c98b0', '300cb464-7371-5fd0-9293-6fcb3f78dddc', 1, true, 'Header–R1, Payload–R2, Trailer–R3.', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"pairs":{"L1":"R1","L2":"R2","L3":"R3"}}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('5ef90f36-5791-5f6a-9b9a-98971a939053', 'ed2014c4-e204-5986-b3d6-714abf899dc6', 1, true, '- A set of rules for communication / data format, sending, receiving, error checking (1)
- so that different devices / manufacturers can communicate and understand each other (1)', '[{"criterion":"Rules","marks":1},{"criterion":"Why","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'A protocol is a set of rules for how data is formatted, sent and checked; all devices follow the same rules, so different computers can communicate.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('a4ac40a6-0379-56c7-ab80-11eb45f412a6', '10dabba5-9ede-5f56-b4db-c7945ae495ba', 1, true, 'L1–R1 … L6–R6.', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"pairs":{"L1":"R1","L2":"R2","L3":"R3","L4":"R4","L5":"R5","L6":"R6"}}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('15a8ef08-ed49-59f4-974a-f0206ebb30bb', 'c523fb2d-4783-58af-a4ae-e945fdd81fd4', 1, true, '- HTTPS encrypts data (SSL/TLS) between browser and server (1)
- so card details / passwords cannot be read if intercepted; certificate proves the site is genuine (1)', '[{"criterion":"Encryption","marks":1},{"criterion":"Protects data / authentication","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'HTTPS encrypts the data, so customers'' card numbers and passwords cannot be read if intercepted, and the certificate shows the shop''s site is genuine.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('b5072fa0-c1ff-5339-a674-7ada501aa222', '782efadd-4427-5b5d-beed-864a7e8e0c8b', 1, true, '- FTP breaks data into packets and re-sends lost or damaged packets (1)
- it reassembles packets arriving in random order before use, causing delays — not suitable for a live stream (1)', '[{"criterion":"Resends","marks":1},{"criterion":"Delay","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'FTP waits for lost or damaged packets to be sent again and puts all packets in order before the file is complete, which creates delays that a live video cannot accept.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('230e142f-97e3-50b6-8bc3-666bd0b9727e', '5daa8b23-c92f-5f7a-b9f0-57be3a980b11', 1, true, 'POP3; IMAP; IMAP (1 each)', '[{"criterion":"Row 1","marks":1},{"criterion":"Row 2","marks":1},{"criterion":"Row 3","marks":1}]'::jsonb, null, '{"rows":[[null,"✓",""],[null,"","✓"],[null,"","✓"]]}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('6a1e17cf-1bff-5dc8-b342-3284337fc222', '412f65ab-ab97-5896-8ede-ba486f4827ea', 1, true, '- TCP checks every packet arrives and resends lost ones — reliable but slower; a file must be complete and correct (1)
- UDP does not resend — fast with little delay; a lost packet in a call is less important than delay (1)', '[{"criterion":"TCP","marks":1},{"criterion":"UDP","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'A file must arrive complete, so TCP resends any lost packet. In a video call a short glitch is better than a delay, so UDP sends packets quickly without resending.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('d9ba7d09-0fcc-52e2-b5b5-2038066c5d85', '690b5e9d-f299-5ac6-b8d3-d1227fc8038e', 1, true, 'Unique address of the device on the network (1); used to send / receive data / identify and locate it (1)', '[{"criterion":"Unique","marks":1},{"criterion":"Send / receive","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'It uniquely identifies the laptop on the network so that packets can be sent to and from it.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('1f54b200-da2e-576f-9c13-1c49380d4eef', 'c579fee0-d178-5c55-8517-12d27e2996e9', 1, true, 'IPv4: 32 bits, four 8-bit numbers 0–255 in denary separated by dots (1). IPv6: 128 bits, eight groups of four hexadecimal digits separated by colons (1)', '[{"criterion":"IPv4","marks":1},{"criterion":"IPv6","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'IPv4: 32 bits written as four denary numbers 0–255 separated by dots. IPv6: 128 bits written as eight groups of four hex digits separated by colons.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('0058f286-262f-5f1e-a3d8-d25cddba3ace', 'b60af4ae-afea-593d-ad30-4005bacd12e6', 1, true, 'Not reachable directly from the Internet / hidden behind the router''s NAT (1); attackers cannot start a connection to the laptop directly (1)', '[{"criterion":"Hidden","marks":1},{"criterion":"No direct attack","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Private addresses cannot be reached from the Internet; the router hides the laptop behind its public address, so attackers cannot connect to it directly.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('eb8cad03-3cea-54dc-82d7-e949e22f6428', 'c95a7f75-2b97-5f35-96cb-fb3fe7579c9f', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): physical vs logical; manufacturer vs network-assigned; fixed vs changes between networks; 48-bit hex vs 32-bit denary; LAN delivery by switches vs routing between networks', '[{"criterion":"1","marks":1},{"criterion":"2","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 The MAC address was set by the manufacturer and does not change, while the IP address was given by the school network and will change at home. 2 The MAC is used by the switch inside the LAN, while the IP is used by routers between networks.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('82d8a05a-6243-5162-a0ae-18d2291302bb', '6300e056-f1db-5bed-9951-5bb348228529', 1, true, 'The router finds the MAC address matching the laptop''s IP (ARP) (1); the switch / access point delivers the frame to the laptop using its MAC address (1)', '[{"criterion":"ARP","marks":1},{"criterion":"Deliver by MAC","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The router looks up which MAC address belongs to 192.168.10.37 and the frame is delivered on the local network to the card with that MAC address.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('6a66c529-f478-5489-be18-404fdb512278', '77a383f5-38b7-5569-aea4-6760de61ff10', 1, true, 'Any three (1 each): source IP address; destination IP address; packet / sequence number; total number of packets; protocol; time to live / hop count', '[{"criterion":"1","marks":1},{"criterion":"2","marks":1},{"criterion":"3","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Source IP address
2 Destination IP address
