- investors must attend reviews regularly (1) which takes a lot of their time (1)
- less documentation (1) so later maintenance may be harder / more expensive (1)
- scope may keep growing (1) increasing cost (1)', '[{"criterion":"Identifies a disadvantage","marks":1},{"criterion":"Explains its effect on the investors","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The total cost and finishing date are hard to predict, because features keep changing after each sprint, so investors cannot be sure how much money they will need.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('a561b7a3-3e3d-5aa2-adf4-157c022aae6d', '347ea515-17fd-585d-8237-135962393b0b', 1, true, 'Client involvement (1): agile — client involved throughout / reviews every sprint; waterfall — mainly at the start and the end

When working software is delivered (1): agile — after every sprint (early and often); waterfall — only at the end of the project', '[{"criterion":"Correct difference in client involvement","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correct difference in delivery of working software","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Client involvement: in agile the client reviews every sprint, in waterfall mainly at the start and end. Delivery: agile delivers working software every sprint, waterfall only at the end.', '{}'::jsonb, 'Each answer must refer to both models (or clearly contrast them).', null),
  ('ce310fe3-9d1a-51e9-b3e5-7c989ab621f3', 'b4e58142-7500-592b-9b25-64796fe25577', 1, true, 'One mark per phase described:
