  ('e1f42e56-93b0-5d4a-9a30-507fe9451a17', '6270f9d8-97df-5f7a-b970-d5f43846836b', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): gestures misinterpreted; accidental / unintended gestures (other staff moving); surgeons must learn the gestures; limited number of commands; tiring; expensive cameras / sensors; affected by lighting; less precise', '[{"criterion":"First valid disadvantage","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second, different valid disadvantage","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Movements of other staff may be detected as commands by mistake. 2 The surgeons have to learn the gestures before they can use it.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('f0570c08-1d42-5a06-abc2-bde09ce6b4a8', 'cab2a860-cc28-5406-96f5-805120edefab', 1, true, 'Up to 3 marks:
- Voice is also touchless / hands-free, and surgeons need not move their hands away from the patient (1)
- But masks muffle speech / machines and staff make noise / medical words may be misinterpreted (1)
- Gestures are not affected by noise / masks, but need space and hand movements (1)
- Justified conclusion (1)
[Max 3]', '[{"criterion":"Valid point about voice in the theatre","marks":1},{"criterion":"Valid point about gestures in the theatre","marks":1},{"criterion":"Justified conclusion","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Voice control would let surgeons keep both hands on the patient, but their masks and the noise of machines may stop the system recognising their words. Gestures are not affected by noise, although the surgeon must move a hand away from the patient. Gesture control is better because noise and masks make voice recognition unreliable.', '{}'::jsonb, 'Either conclusion can score full marks if justified for the operating theatre.', null),
  ('0dac28d9-cf9e-5ee8-afee-05cdaa41d6f5', '00602e61-1a34-521a-9cd3-8bc6e4b87a6c', 1, true, 'Up to 4 marks:
- GUI (touch): intuitive, icons and maps, no training, can show pictures of exhibits (1); but touched by many people / hygiene (1)
- NLI: visitors can ask questions naturally / helps visually impaired (1); but noise / accents / many languages (1)
- GRI: touchless / engaging (1); but gestures must be learnt / limited commands (1)
- Justified conclusion linked to the museum (1)
[Max 4]', '[{"criterion":"Valid point about one interface","marks":1},{"criterion":"Valid point about another interface","marks":1},{"criterion":"A drawback linked to the museum / visitors","marks":1},{"criterion":"Justified conclusion","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'A touch-screen GUI is intuitive: visitors of any age can tap icons and see pictures and maps of the exhibits without training. A natural language interface would let visitors ask questions aloud, but a busy museum is noisy and visitors speak different languages, so it may misunderstand them. A gesture interface is touchless, but visitors would need to learn the gestures. The GUI is most suitable because it needs no learning and can display images of the exhibits.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('250b8675-303e-532d-bccc-1ba17fff8cb1', 'd127dc42-b03c-5481-a0bd-dce12f2dd9c7', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each) / reason + explanation: visitors do not know the commands; commands need exact syntax / typing errors; not intuitive for children / elderly; text only — cannot show pictures of exhibits; needs a keyboard', '[{"criterion":"Valid reason","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second reason or explanation","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Visitors would not know the commands or their exact syntax, and a CLI shows only text, so it could not display pictures or maps of the exhibits.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
