  ('c45cec59-a8bb-5eb1-a3dc-3b9953cf576a', '9805d409-8924-57a1-9c17-797f55127611', 1, true, 'A4-5E-60-C2-7D-10 (1)', '[{"criterion":"MAC","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'A4-5E-60-C2-7D-10', '{"values":["A4-5E-60-C2-7D-10","A4:5E:60:C2:7D:10","A45E60C27D10"],"ignore_spaces":true}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('790cbe32-399c-565c-8104-4efafede4e17', '73c621cd-e0e8-5d31-b5d4-07a735d88c98', 1, true, 'A MAC address belongs to a network interface card, not the whole computer (1); the laptop has two NICs (wireless and wired), each with its own unique MAC (1)', '[{"criterion":"MAC per NIC","marks":1},{"criterion":"Two NICs","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Each network interface has its own MAC address; the laptop has a wireless card and an Ethernet card, so it has two.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('8eb8607d-8639-5de9-a485-57d5ab7e9617', '6599af82-6bde-5bbd-a7a8-f8ac9d2f7060', 1, true, 'Each difference: circuit side (1) + packet side (1). E.g. addresses only at set-up vs in each packet; dedicated channel / in order vs different routes / reassembled; reserved vs shared bandwidth; lost vs rerouted on failure', '[{"criterion":"Difference 1 circuit","marks":1},{"criterion":"Difference 1 packet","marks":1},{"criterion":"Difference 2 circuit","marks":1},{"criterion":"Difference 2 packet","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Circuit switching needs the sender and recipient addresses only when the connection is set up, whereas in packet switching each packet carries both addresses. 2 Circuit switching sends data in order along one dedicated channel, whereas packet switching sends packets by different routes and reassembles them.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('f0fed0f1-95ad-5ee9-be6d-17528a0f0625', '2be1fe61-9c2f-5a87-a831-8d5412841d01', 1, true, 'Any four (1 each): message split into packets; header with source / destination IP and sequence number; routers read destination IP and forward each packet; packets may take different routes / arrive out of order; receiver checks for errors / requests missing packets; packets reassembled in order', '[{"criterion":"1","marks":1},{"criterion":"2","marks":1},{"criterion":"3","marks":1},{"criterion":"4","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The e-mail is split into packets, each with the source and destination IP addresses and a sequence number. Routers forward each packet on the best route available, so they may travel different ways. The receiver checks them, asks for any missing ones and reassembles the e-mail using the sequence numbers.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('ce8385d6-c1f2-515f-b99e-075de9c286a8', 'a34c191e-b868-5cdc-af2d-f4d7baaf1415', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): picture and sound not synchronised; interruptions / video not continuous; degraded by competing traffic', '[{"criterion":"1","marks":1},{"criterion":"2","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Picture and sound are not synchronised. 2 The video is interrupted when the network is busy.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('5e4335f0-8d34-5587-92d0-9f2e854f4c5f', '562462d2-7d76-57a8-acb0-46bb0d59637c', 1, true, 'Reserved path gives constant low delay / guaranteed bandwidth, so speech is continuous and in order (1)', '[{"criterion":"Constant delay / guaranteed","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The reserved circuit gives a constant, low delay, so speech arrives continuously and in order.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('7fa9cf1b-cc9c-52fb-bd47-a622f0e6d008', 'a7228ac8-0915-5b5a-8c91-ace1c0dce10f', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): physical vs network / logical address; assigned by manufacturer vs assigned by the network; identifies the device vs identifies its location / network; fixed vs changes; 48-bit hex vs 32-bit denary', '[{"criterion":"1","marks":1},{"criterion":"2","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 A MAC address is a physical address set by the manufacturer, while an IP address is a network address given when the device joins a network. 2 The MAC address identifies the device''s network card, while the IP address identifies where the device is on the network.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('64724b77-9e50-5b20-9734-2cd7d7cb56f9', 'f6905590-9ba4-5585-96b5-9659be211f38', 1, true, 'Destination IP 185.22.66.10 stays the same for the whole journey (1); MAC addresses change at each hop (1); destination MAC is always the next router / finally the server; source MAC is the device sending on that link (1)', '[{"criterion":"IP same","marks":1},{"criterion":"MAC changes","marks":1},{"criterion":"Next hop explained","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The destination IP address 185.22.66.10 stays the same. The MAC addresses change at every hop: each frame is addressed from the device sending it to the next router, and on the last link to the web server.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('5806dec6-9235-5998-b1ce-08563df872d7', 'ef147685-b6bd-595d-a325-36e8ce42f435', 1, true, 'ARP request broadcast asking for the MAC of the router''s IP (1); router replies with its MAC, stored in ARP cache (1)', '[{"criterion":"Broadcast","marks":1},{"criterion":"Reply","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'It broadcasts an ARP request asking which device has the router''s IP address; the router replies with its MAC address, which the laptop stores in its ARP cache.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
