- Imperative programs change state using variables / assignment whereas declarative programs have little or no changing state
- Declarative programs are usually shorter / less efficient; imperative programs give more control / can be optimised', '[{"criterion":"First valid difference","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second, different valid difference","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Declarative programming focuses on what the program should achieve, whereas imperative programming focuses on how to achieve it. 2 An imperative program is a clearly defined sequence of instructions, whereas in a declarative program the order of execution is decided by the system.', '{}'::jsonb, 'Each box must compare both paradigms (or make the other side clear). Do not award the same idea twice.', null),
  ('0966546e-99c3-5ca3-a15d-627e5defecae', '10aa415d-b6e6-5145-a556-b5dab76ca597', 1, true, 'A (SQL) → Declarative (1); B (Python loop) → Imperative (1); C (Prolog) → Declarative (1); D (assembly) → Imperative (1)', '[{"criterion":"A declarative","marks":1},{"criterion":"B imperative","marks":1},{"criterion":"C declarative","marks":1},{"criterion":"D imperative","marks":1}]'::jsonb, null, '{"rows":[[null,"","✓"],[null,"✓",""],[null,"","✓"],[null,"✓",""]]}'::jsonb, null, 'SQL and Prolog describe the result / facts and rules. The Python loop and the assembly code give a sequence of steps that change state.'),
