  ('615252cf-ecd8-5e1f-8a96-87d9201b11f8', '97699bb9-66de-5283-9872-df08664e8160', 1, true, 'Up to 6 marks, from:
- Imperative: sequence of instructions saying how; programmer controls the order (1)
- Imperative: variables / state changed by assignment (1)
- Declarative: describes what — facts, rules, queries; the system decides how / execution order not delineated (1)
- Declarative: little or no changing state (1)
- Advantage/disadvantage of imperative: control and efficiency / longer code, more errors (1)
- Advantage/disadvantage of declarative: shorter, easier for searching / less control, may be less efficient (1)
- Correct example of each: e.g. Python / C / Java and Prolog / SQL / Haskell (1)
[Max 6]', '[{"criterion":"How imperative programs are written / executed","marks":1},{"criterion":"How declarative programs are written / executed","marks":1},{"criterion":"State / variables difference","marks":1},{"criterion":"Advantage or disadvantage of imperative","marks":1},{"criterion":"Advantage or disadvantage of declarative","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correct example language for each","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'In an imperative language such as Python, the program is a sequence of instructions that tells the computer how to solve the problem; the programmer controls the order of execution and the values of variables change as it runs. In a declarative language such as Prolog or SQL, the programmer describes what is required using facts, rules or queries, and the language system decides how to find the result, with little or no changing state. Imperative programs give the programmer full control, so they can be optimised for speed, but they are usually longer and errors in changing variables are harder to find. Declarative programs are shorter and suit searching data or expert systems, but the programmer has less control, so they may be less efficient.', '{}'::jsonb, 'A comparison point needs both sides. Award the example mark only when an example of each paradigm is correct.', null),
