  ('c432b1bb-8ce2-5a08-bca0-0eb7090d5018', 'ba786acc-142c-572f-b9af-29182fb22772', 1, true, 'Natural Language: ambiguous, leading to misinterpretation; struggles with colloquial language, homophones or context-based queries; users may need to learn specific commands / syntax (1)
Gesture: misread / unintended gestures; must be learnt; tiring; limited (1)', '[{"criterion":"NLI","marks":1},{"criterion":"GRI","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Natural language: it may misunderstand homophones. Gesture recognition: waving to someone may change the channel by accident.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('2557427d-edbe-53ab-9be9-7b1f98bb3b3e', '9d89aa46-e42b-5dce-9d69-dae9ad5f79fd', 1, true, 'Any three (1 each): microphone records; speech recognition → text; NLP finds intent (turn off) and entity (kitchen lights); command sent to the lights; confirmation by speech synthesis', '[{"criterion":"First","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second","marks":1},{"criterion":"Third","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Speech recognition turns the voice into text; natural-language processing identifies the intent "turn off" and the device "kitchen lights"; the speaker sends the command to the lights and confirms by speech synthesis.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('acc82cd2-230a-5598-98ff-d0a2fb166e10', '594886eb-db53-5d12-86a7-deec2f9835ea', 1, true, 'Advantage (1) + explanation (1): no need to read a screen / learn commands — speaks naturally and hears answers', '[{"criterion":"Advantage","marks":1},{"criterion":"Explanation","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'She can simply speak to the device, so she does not need to read small text or find buttons on a screen.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('60fff481-e9d1-5405-b50b-9a94f29b52a0', '039bbb0a-9e88-5424-bca2-28d02c9c5889', 1, true, 'Always listening / recordings stored / shared with company / could be hacked (1)', '[{"criterion":"Valid concern","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'It is always listening, so private conversations may be recorded.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('5dbbb013-3d34-5c70-96a3-183684e7ea9d', '38dfd4bd-33b0-5e4a-ae5d-0f9bdfdc373d', 1, true, 'Up to 4: touchscreen — familiar but touching breaks sterility (1); voice — hands-free but masks / machine noise / medical terms misinterpreted (1); gesture — touchless, sterile, not affected by noise, but must be learnt / accidental movements (1); justified recommendation (1)', '[{"criterion":"GUI","marks":1},{"criterion":"Voice","marks":1},{"criterion":"Gesture","marks":1},{"criterion":"Recommendation","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'A touchscreen is familiar, but touching it would contaminate the surgeon''s sterile gloves. Voice control is hands-free, but masks and the noise of machines may cause misinterpretation. Gestures are touchless and work in noise, although surgeons must learn them and accidental movements may change the image. Gesture recognition is most suitable because it keeps hands sterile and is not affected by noise.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('09c57e5b-0826-510e-8abf-3d49b07d1c08', 'fc709b81-ff71-5f31-8c3d-cfa5375831aa', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): camera / sensor captures movement; hand tracked; compared with stored gesture patterns; matching gesture triggers command', '[{"criterion":"First","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'A depth camera tracks the surgeon''s hand, and the software compares the movement with stored gesture patterns; a match triggers the linked command, such as next scan.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('dd43c2cf-1768-53f7-86b8-e15571559222', 'a15341e1-b8b1-5885-bd02-7cb441872f0c', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): consistency; feedback; error prevention / recovery; recognition rather than recall; efficiency; accessibility', '[{"criterion":"First","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Feedback — show clearly that a gesture was recognised. 2 Error recovery — an easy way to go back to the previous scan.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('d6291b19-fe6b-5949-8d29-d288f5e88a20', '69aea490-8878-56d9-a027-2fdcc56abecb', 1, true, '- A computer-generated 3D environment that completely replaces the user''s view of the real world (1)
- in which the user feels immersed / can interact in real time, usually with a headset (1)', '[{"criterion":"Computer-generated environment replacing the real view","marks":1},{"criterion":"Immersion / interaction","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'A computer-generated 3D environment that completely replaces what the user sees, so that, wearing a headset, they feel present in it and can interact with it.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('6dcafb29-bd28-578a-bea4-ae1e505451eb', 'c46f5af3-f1c7-5d1f-bb11-d45b6ab4f308', 1, true, '- Computer-generated content (images, text, 3D objects) is added / overlaid on the user''s view of the real world (1)
- in real time; the real world remains visible (via phone camera / AR glasses) (1)', '[{"criterion":"Digital content overlaid on the real world","marks":1},{"criterion":"Real time / real world still visible","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Technology that adds computer-generated images and information on top of the user''s live view of the real world, which they can still see.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('e678812d-f023-50a5-8edc-0749fc2526c0', 'de69b1ad-504c-58bf-ab2c-3bc00163b94a', 1, true, 'A virtual reality replaces your vision, augmented reality adds to it (1). VR completely covers and replaces your field of vision; AR apps only show digital objects on top of the real world on your screen / glasses (1)', '[{"criterion":"VR replaces the view","marks":1},{"criterion":"AR adds to the real view","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Virtual reality replaces the user''s vision with a computer-generated world, whereas augmented reality adds digital objects on top of the real world that the user can still see.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('73d57af2-b6e2-52c7-b094-fd55ec9cb64f', '5de5d697-f30c-5a79-b7fd-86787dbfeb02', 1, true, 'B', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"correct":"B"}'::jsonb, null, 'The translation is drawn over the live camera view of the real sign.'),
  ('2374eab7-36fa-5d28-869b-8a341ef24abe', '88233bae-33b4-53d1-9373-a1be7cab03c1', 1, true, 'Space station → VR (1); Windscreen → AR (1); Robot behind sofa → MR (1); Skyscraper therapy → VR (1)', '[{"criterion":"Row 1 VR","marks":1},{"criterion":"Row 2 AR","marks":1},{"criterion":"Row 3 MR","marks":1},{"criterion":"Row 4 VR","marks":1}]'::jsonb, null, '{"rows":[[null,"✓","",""],[null,"","✓",""],[null,"","","✓"],[null,"✓","",""]]}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('4b75c79c-b351-5092-a44c-f74750e72b81', 'c6a7af05-83e7-5e21-aeb6-0479511f7e0a', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): provides real-time access to information / data / context relevant to the physical world; provides immersive and interactive learning experiences; try products before buying; navigation / directions; maintenance or repair instructions over equipment; guidance in surgery; entertainment / games; tourism information', '[{"criterion":"First valid purpose","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second, different valid purpose","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 To give real-time information about what the user is looking at, such as directions in a city. 2 To provide interactive learning, such as a 3D model of the heart on a textbook page.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('ef74e1c2-289f-5e89-a74d-44570a5df187', 'c5690318-60e3-5255-9f61-f2d61586e1d5', 1, true, 'L1–R1, L2–R2, L3–R3, L4–R4. One mark per correct match.', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"pairs":{"L1":"R1","L2":"R2","L3":"R3","L4":"R4"}}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('a0f4c00e-6611-559c-8632-623bdfa24960', 'c697e764-847e-5e72-92b3-a0fc7be62fd5', 1, true, 'A and C. One mark each; a mark is lost for each incorrect option selected.', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"correct":["A","C"]}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('06dfae98-681a-5d50-a435-4859a80003e9', '58569c14-15d1-5666-97e1-c5fc7b0032b1', 1, true, 'Any three (1 each): two screens / a different image for each eye give stereoscopic 3D; the display fills the field of view / blocks the real world; sensors track head movement and update the view instantly; high frame rate / low latency; controllers let the user interact; spatial audio; haptic feedback', '[{"criterion":"First valid point","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second valid point","marks":1},{"criterion":"Third valid point","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The headset shows a slightly different image to each eye, which creates 3D depth, and it blocks out the real world. Sensors track the user''s head, so the view changes immediately when they look around, and spatial sound comes from the direction of virtual objects.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('d44afe4f-e57a-58bb-814c-9635cfd0cd51', 'ec325bc9-a9c4-5986-94aa-3a025f922b11', 1, true, 'Any three (1 each): camera captures live video; sensors / computer vision detect the table surface and the phone''s position; the 3D model is anchored to the table; the lamp is drawn over the video at the correct size and angle; it is updated as the phone moves so it stays in place', '[{"criterion":"First valid step","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second valid step","marks":1},{"criterion":"Third valid step","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The camera captures live video of the room, and computer vision detects the flat surface of the table. The app anchors the 3D lamp to the table and draws it over the video at the right size; as the phone moves, sensors update the view so the lamp stays in place.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('50557526-250b-5709-8946-ada0932faaef', '0571f43a-fac2-5b04-8135-753a68a9527e', 1, true, 'Marker–R1, Location–R2, Markerless–R3. One mark per correct match.', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"pairs":{"L1":"R1","L2":"R2","L3":"R3"}}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('b3badeb2-bdb0-55c2-88c3-05c5d89dd57b', '20ccafa2-5d4e-5ed1-8ec0-3f950468f257', 1, true, '- Firefighters can practise entering burning buildings / rescues in a realistic simulation (1)
- without danger / cheaper than real fires / any scenario can be repeated (1)', '[{"criterion":"Realistic practice of dangerous situations","marks":1},{"criterion":"Safe / cheaper / repeatable","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Firefighters can practise searching a burning building in a realistic virtual environment, without any real danger, and repeat the exercise as many times as needed.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('f4f0835f-c99e-572a-877f-138aca074377', 'de489706-2d1a-5ccf-9fcb-dbe5cbbd0955', 1, true, 'VR (1): surgery practice / training; treating phobias / PTSD; pain distraction; rehabilitation exercises; explaining an operation to a patient
AR (1): showing veins / scans / organs over the patient''s body during surgery; guiding injections; remote expert guidance; anatomy teaching on a real body', '[{"criterion":"Valid VR purpose","marks":1},{"criterion":"Valid AR purpose","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'VR: treating patients'' fear of flying with a virtual flight. AR: showing a patient''s CT scan over their body during an operation.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('ef937c81-c44b-5170-9cf8-87c9af29cb4e', '0cb1af4d-ded1-5b58-9b52-934325ca414b', 1, true, '- Customers can see a 3D model of the furniture in their own room at its real size (1)
- so they know it fits / matches before buying → more sales / fewer returns (1)', '[{"criterion":"Try the product in the real room","marks":1},{"criterion":"Benefit: confidence / fewer returns / more sales","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Customers can place a life-size 3D model of a sofa in their own living room, so they can check that it fits and looks right before buying, which means fewer returns for the shop.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('dbf0afe3-e714-5199-944f-65420067e2a3', 'ad2e829f-ba4f-50b8-a3fe-c5940c2b81f3', 1, true, 'True.', '[]'::jsonb, null, '{"correct":true}'::jsonb, null, 'A delay between head movement and the image (latency) or a low frame rate confuses the brain and causes motion sickness.'),
  ('0d6ee7eb-4363-55e8-8a3a-3978f673b1c8', '7d23c816-a76b-5f83-ad79-578fcf3a7281', 1, true, 'VR (1): motion sickness / eye strain; isolated from surroundings / may bump into objects; expensive equipment; needs powerful computer
AR (1): distraction — dangerous while walking / driving; privacy — camera records surroundings; drains battery; small screen; tracking errors; AR glasses expensive', '[{"criterion":"Valid VR drawback","marks":1},{"criterion":"Valid AR drawback","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'VR: users cannot see the real room, so they may trip over furniture. AR: people may walk into traffic while looking at their phone.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('e72728d5-2a06-5d3b-b4b4-7b802b870c44', '5e8e7fa2-1cae-5d0e-8ebd-193fd9f9497c', 1, true, 'VR (max 2): full immersion — virtual trips inside cells / to space; safe experiments; but expensive, motion sickness, isolates students / teacher cannot see what they see, limited time per lesson
AR (max 2): 3D models over textbooks / desk; students still see the teacher and each other — collaboration; tablets cheaper / multi-use; but less immersive, small screen
Justified conclusion (1)
[Max 4]', '[{"criterion":"Valid point about VR in lessons","marks":1},{"criterion":"Valid point about AR in lessons","marks":1},{"criterion":"A drawback of one option","marks":1},{"criterion":"Justified conclusion","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'VR headsets would give a fully immersive experience, such as travelling inside a cell, but they are expensive, can cause motion sickness and cut students off from the teacher. Tablets with AR show 3D models on top of the textbook while students can still see and talk to each other, and the tablets can be used for other tasks too. The school should choose the AR tablets because they are cheaper, more flexible and allow group work.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('6d7ff173-35d5-587f-a4b9-31ca32230bf2', '85f0e0d0-d3d6-53ff-921e-ebf2790db369', 1, true, '- The app uses GPS / compass to know where the visitor is and which part of the building they are looking at (1)
- and shows information / history / reconstructions / directions over the camera view (1)', '[{"criterion":"GPS / position detection","marks":1},{"criterion":"Relevant content shown over the real view","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Using GPS, the app knows which part of the mausoleum the visitor is pointing the phone at and shows its history and a reconstruction of how it looked when it was built.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('90a15ec7-4aed-5582-833d-c128ca9d4cf7', '2109f016-b25a-5ac1-9564-57f61e9cd403', 1, true, '- People who cannot travel can experience / explore the building in 3D from anywhere (1)
- it advertises the site so more people decide to visit / shows closed or fragile areas (1)', '[{"criterion":"Remote experience","marks":1},{"criterion":"Benefit: attracts visitors / access to closed areas","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'People anywhere in the world can explore the mausoleum in 3D, which advertises it and encourages them to come and visit in person.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('3a1353f0-b4d5-595f-93cc-a58f744bc6b2', 'e31369f4-1d9f-580b-bc77-3226958cafde', 1, true, 'Problem (1) + explanation (1): distraction — visitors look at the screen and trip / walk into others; battery drains quickly; weak GPS / mobile signal so content appears in the wrong place; small screen hard to see in bright sunlight', '[{"criterion":"Valid problem","marks":1},{"criterion":"Explanation","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'In bright sunlight the phone screen is hard to see, and visitors who keep looking at it may trip on the steps.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('3a4f959a-f751-537f-99fa-adc82b1fe5e2', 'f9ac80e2-9b8c-5810-aa53-23bb7ee0d9c8', 1, true, 'VR (max 3): purpose / example (simulators for pilots, surgeons; virtual field trips) (1); benefits — safe, cheaper, repeatable, engaging (1); drawbacks — cost, motion sickness, isolation (1)
AR (max 3): purpose / example (3D models over textbooks, instructions over machines) (1); benefits — real world visible, cheap on phones, collaboration (1); drawbacks — distraction, small screen, less immersive (1)
A conclusion may gain credit within the maximum.', '[{"criterion":"VR purpose with example","marks":1},{"criterion":"VR benefit","marks":1},{"criterion":"VR drawback","marks":1},{"criterion":"AR purpose with example","marks":1},{"criterion":"AR benefit","marks":1},{"criterion":"AR drawback","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'VR is used for simulators in which pilots and surgeons practise dangerous procedures. This is safe, cheaper than real practice and can be repeated, but headsets are expensive, some users feel sick and they are cut off from the real world. AR is used to show 3D models of organs or planets over textbooks and instructions over real machines. Learners still see the real world and can work together, and it runs on phones most people already own, but it is less immersive and the small screen can be distracting. VR is best for realistic practice of dangerous tasks, and AR for everyday classroom learning.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null)
