CU: manages the execution of instructions and sends control signals to the other components.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('56376e50-a869-53e6-a884-9a334778aa56', '49c03fda-1578-5673-8707-c6a4b67069d8', 1, true, 'Any four (1 each): PC → MAR; address on the address bus; memory read signal on the control bus; instruction on the data bus to the MDR; MDR → CIR; PC incremented', '[{"criterion":"1","marks":1},{"criterion":"2","marks":1},{"criterion":"3","marks":1},{"criterion":"4","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The address in the PC is copied to the MAR and sent on the address bus; a memory-read signal is sent on the control bus; the instruction returns on the data bus into the MDR and is copied to the CIR; the PC is incremented.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('d8897011-94cd-5060-a375-40d360faf6d2', '945eb5c7-2bcf-5ffb-8e5b-a516b140a104', 1, true, 'Input; output; output; storage — 2 rows correct = 1 mark, 4 rows = 2 marks', '[{"criterion":"Two rows","marks":1},{"criterion":"Four rows","marks":1}]'::jsonb, null, '{"rows":[[null,"✓","",""],[null,"","✓",""],[null,"","✓",""],[null,"","","✓"]]}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('f38e89f9-3c6d-51ed-90be-e74d35535d8a', 'b3aa2d9e-4a84-593c-bfb0-627537433464', 1, true, 'The driver is software that lets the OS communicate with this specific printer (1); it translates general OS commands into the signals / commands the printer understands (1)', '[{"criterion":"OS communication","marks":1},{"criterion":"Translation","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The operating system does not know the commands of every printer model; the driver translates its general print commands into the exact signals this printer understands.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('9183e3d8-c9ae-51ca-ab2c-47ebbae3b541', 'e7aba39c-0533-5e85-aed3-e50090736df3', 1, true, 'CPU checks for interrupts at the end of the current cycle (1); if priority is higher, saves registers / current state (1); runs the interrupt service routine (e.g. displays ''out of paper''), then restores registers and continues (1)', '[{"criterion":"Check end of cycle","marks":1},{"criterion":"Save state","marks":1},{"criterion":"ISR and restore","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'At the end of the current fetch–decode–execute cycle the CPU sees the interrupt, saves its registers, runs the interrupt service routine that shows an ''out of paper'' message, then restores the registers and continues the previous task.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('4f587c83-04a5-5b44-b963-708f3d91f30e', '71a5904c-c481-5e9c-b2cb-f320eb4015b3', 1, true, 'CPU sends data to the buffer and returns to other tasks (1); printer prints from the buffer at its own speed (1); printer sends an interrupt when the buffer is empty so the CPU refills it (1)', '[{"criterion":"Fill and return","marks":1},{"criterion":"Printer own speed","marks":1},{"criterion":"Interrupt refill","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The CPU puts part of the document into the buffer and returns to the teacher''s work; the printer prints from the buffer at its own slow speed and sends an interrupt when it is empty, so the CPU refills it.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('e6e940cb-eccd-518d-9b7f-e0b8bfcdbd0c', '7a14f796-04e8-5240-a032-c45abbda13e4', 1, true, 'Memory that loses its contents when the power is turned off (1)', '[{"criterion":"Definition","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Memory that loses its contents when the power is switched off.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('5804da05-641d-5e7a-a994-c3c993617994', '8876ab07-a1a3-5978-b258-1184698c55f4', 1, true, 'Any three, both sides (1 each): volatile vs non-volatile; read/write vs read only; running programs and data vs boot program / firmware; large vs small capacity; contents change constantly vs set by manufacturer', '[{"criterion":"1","marks":1},{"criterion":"2","marks":1},{"criterion":"3","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 RAM is volatile, whereas ROM keeps its contents without power. 2 RAM can be read and written, whereas ROM is normally only read. 3 RAM holds the running programs and data, whereas ROM holds the boot program.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('8faf03ee-e4b2-5080-a9ed-2dcdc23beff7', '9bd47368-9d48-5d37-8e48-1aafd468a8bf', 1, true, 'To store the user''s input (1), e.g. the cooking time and power level chosen, which change each time (1)', '[{"criterion":"User input","marks":1},{"criterion":"Example","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'To store the user''s input, such as the cooking time and heat setting, which is different every time the oven is used.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('a7e0ff9e-13b9-57f4-aced-a2132472a68f', '23b4eb6a-7249-51b2-9694-6c9c36d7901f', 1, true, 'Frees up space in RAM (1); increases the memory available beyond physical RAM / allows more programs to run at once (1)', '[{"criterion":"Frees RAM","marks":1},{"criterion":"More memory / tasks","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'It frees up space in RAM by moving unused pages to the disk, so more memory is available and more programs can run at the same time.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('aa5546b7-7c0e-5109-acb6-e41138ce19d9', '1da8d4f5-95a5-5c3f-9abb-c2b9f6924325', 1, true, 'System slowdown (1) because much time is spent moving data between RAM and disk / disk is slower than RAM (1); OR increased wear on the HDD/SSD (1) because of frequent reads and writes (1)', '[{"criterion":"Problem","marks":1},{"criterion":"Explanation","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The computer slows down, because a lot of time is spent moving data between RAM and the disk, which is much slower than RAM.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('b3657fbf-9b39-5dd0-8070-6d1cdfe1f750', '5d9705b9-a5ce-5473-ad78-eb5f0a3b1cbd', 1, true, 'Stores copies of frequently used data / instructions (1) so the CPU does not have to wait for slower RAM / faster access (1)', '[{"criterion":"Frequently used","marks":1},{"criterion":"Avoids RAM","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Cache holds copies of frequently used instructions and data close to the CPU, so they can be accessed faster than from RAM.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('e596ba41-db3f-5130-b79b-0021dc0a99a6', 'b7b13952-cd93-5fe4-a154-bb9dee9aef5e', 1, true, 'Cache closer to / in the CPU (1), reducing access time (1); OR smaller, so faster to search (1)(1); OR uses SRAM with lower latency (1)(1)', '[{"criterion":"Reason","marks":1},{"criterion":"Explanation","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Cache is located inside the CPU, so the data travels a shorter distance and is reached in less time than RAM.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('051bc43d-859f-5c53-881b-030474adf42f', 'cff98917-0a7c-5da7-8bb3-13defe84193e', 1, true, 'CPU checks the cache first; hit — data found and used immediately (1); miss — data fetched from RAM and a copy placed in the cache (1)', '[{"criterion":"Hit","marks":1},{"criterion":"Miss","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The CPU looks in the cache first. On a hit the data is used straight away; on a miss it is fetched from RAM and a copy is stored in the cache for next time.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('fefbacc7-8551-5a18-890a-499c248e979e', '1ebc1bc4-654f-5d84-893d-8aa46cc1a2af', 1, true, 'Processor B (1); larger cache holds more data → more hits, fewer slow RAM accesses (1)', '[{"criterion":"B","marks":1},{"criterion":"Reason","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Processor B: its larger cache holds more data and instructions, so there are more cache hits and fewer slow accesses to RAM.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('480c341a-0786-5da8-b74d-d00ed64b567b', '27ff9380-2712-5538-a20c-5c82b1ad6130', 1, true, 'Registers, cache, RAM, SSD — 2 correct = 1 mark, all correct = 2 marks', '[{"criterion":"Two","marks":1},{"criterion":"All","marks":1}]'::jsonb, null, '{"rows":[[null,"registers||register"],[null,"cache"],[null,"RAM||main memory"],[null,"SSD"]]}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('d929a200-1a86-5007-800c-d91f4401ef05', '3699b89b-a2cc-59c1-8a6e-eb45278408d2', 1, true, 'B: `Low ← Middle + 1` (1)', '[{"criterion":"Selects the correct update for searching the upper half of the array.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'B', '{"correct":"B"}'::jsonb, '', 'If the target is greater than the middle value in an ascending array, all values at or below `Middle` can be discarded. The next search interval starts at `Middle + 1`.'),
  ('48355574-6fc8-5125-86d0-c2ad58151281', '4fe91eab-1187-5521-9117-39a740ddd139', 1, true, 'False (1)', '[{"criterion":"States that the statement is false.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'False', '{"correct":false}'::jsonb, '', 'Binary search depends on comparing the target with the middle value and discarding half of the remaining data. This only works if the data is sorted into a known order.'),
  ('503aeefa-f7a4-594c-a60a-d6ed4993fd32', '33b06714-168d-5edb-872b-d416a61fe699', 1, true, 'L1 matched to R1 (1)
L2 matched to R2 (1)
L3 matched to R3 (1)
L4 matched to R4 (1)', '[{"criterion":"Correctly matches bubble sort to adjacent comparisons and swaps.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly matches insertion sort to inserting into a sorted section.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly matches binary search to middle item and halving.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly matches linear search to sequential checking.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'L1-R1, L2-R2, L3-R3, L4-R4', '{"pairs":{"L1":"R1","L2":"R2","L3":"R3","L4":"R4"}}'::jsonb, '', 'Bubble sort works by adjacent swaps. Insertion sort places the current item into the already sorted part of the list, while binary search repeatedly halves a sorted search space.'),
  ('fa249aa5-a569-545d-8758-ef9b20138330', '5ef7d358-dea2-5ce8-ad1c-6419eadaf322', 1, true, '`N - 1` for the outer loop limit (1)
`N - Pass` for the inner loop limit (1)
`>` as the comparison for ascending order (1)

Blank (2): accept N - 1 (correct but checks the already-sorted end of the array).', '[{"criterion":"Uses `N - 1` as the final outer pass.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses `N - Pass` as the final adjacent comparison position.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses `>` so larger values are swapped towards the end.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'FOR Pass ← 1 TO N - 1
    FOR Index ← 1 TO N - Pass
        IF Values[Index] > Values[Index + 1] THEN', '{"blanks":[["N - 1","N-1","N – 1"],["N - Pass","N-Pass","N – Pass","N - 1","N-1","N – 1"],[">"]]}'::jsonb, '', 'Bubble sort needs at most `N - 1` passes. After each pass, the largest remaining value has moved to the end, so the inner loop can stop at `N - Pass`.'),
  ('f5b3d2c5-c529-5a69-b366-1a717d82e4d6', '7d5ac476-b3fe-5e3b-976f-c227a6c84142', 1, true, 'A: stores the current item before shifting values (1)
C: shifts larger items in the sorted section to the right (1)', '[{"criterion":"Identifies storing the current item as part of insertion sort.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Identifies shifting larger items right as part of insertion sort.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'A and C', '{"correct":["A","C"]}'::jsonb, '', 'Insertion sort keeps a sorted section at the start of the array. The current item is stored, larger sorted items are shifted right, and the current item is inserted into the gap.'),
  ('c8f5a089-9403-5453-ba7c-293f48e88d9f', '8517da83-d4af-531f-b941-70d8bf96614f', 1, true, 'After Pass 1: `1, 2, 4, 1, 3, 5` (1)
After Pass 2: `2, 2, 1, 3, 4, 5` (1)', '[{"criterion":"Correct row after the first pass.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correct row after the second pass.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '| Pass | A[1] | A[2] | A[3] | A[4] | A[5] |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | 2 | 4 | 1 | 3 | 5 |
| 2 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 4 | 5 |', '{"rows":[["1","2","4","1","3","5"],["2","2","1","3","4","5"]]}'::jsonb, '', 'At the end of the first bubble pass, the largest value, 5, has moved to the final position. The second pass bubbles 4 into the fourth position.'),
  ('7a5cb75e-6e98-5a64-9a08-6bcd9db85d24', 'b86307e4-d315-5748-8658-2ad1bc750d32', 1, true, 'Uses a `WHILE` loop that continues while `Position > 1` (1)
Also tests `Data[Position - 1] > CurrentValue` in the `WHILE` condition (1)
Moves `Data[Position - 1]` to `Data[Position]` inside the loop (1)
Decrements `Position` inside the loop (1)
Places `CurrentValue` into `Data[Position]` after the loop (1)', '[{"criterion":"Includes `WHILE Position > 1` as a boundary check.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Includes comparison with the preceding value, `Data[Position - 1] > CurrentValue`.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly shifts the preceding value right.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly decreases `Position` within the loop.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Inserts the stored current value at the final position.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'FOR CurrentIndex ← 2 TO N
    CurrentValue ← Data[CurrentIndex]
    Position ← CurrentIndex
    WHILE Position > 1 AND Data[Position - 1] > CurrentValue
        Data[Position] ← Data[Position - 1]
        Position ← Position - 1
    ENDWHILE
    Data[Position] ← CurrentValue
NEXT CurrentIndex', '{}'::jsonb, 'Award equivalent Cambridge-style pseudocode. Accept a `REPEAT` structure only if it preserves the boundary check and does not access `Data[0]`. Do not award the comparison mark if the comparison sorts into descending order.', 'Insertion sort stores the item being inserted so that other values can be shifted over it. Once no earlier value is greater, or the start of the array is reached, the stored item is inserted into the gap.'),
  ('086c2191-e783-5a14-aa35-6f19ba850dc1', '1d98b824-ea27-522f-b2bd-45b12082f1c3', 1, true, 'Binary search compares the target value with the middle item (1)
It decides which half of the array cannot contain the target and discards that half (1)
This decision is only valid when all values on one side of the middle are known to be smaller or larger because the array is sorted (1)', '[{"criterion":"Refers to comparing the target with the middle item.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Explains that half of the search interval is discarded.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Explains that sorted order makes the discard decision valid.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Binary search tests the middle item of the current search interval. If the target is smaller or larger, it discards one half of the interval. This is only safe if the array is sorted, because then all values on one side of the middle are known to be lower or higher than the middle value.', '{}'::jsonb, 'Credit equivalent wording. Do not credit answers that only say ''because it is faster'' without explaining why sorting is required.', 'The key property of binary search is that a comparison with the middle item gives information about an entire half of the list. Without sorted order, the target might be in either half.'),
  ('84dcf865-f2ab-5200-afaf-bfd19925730a', 'c7bbb70e-2c41-5156-a7d1-e22d4fea13c3', 1, true, 'Shows or uses powers of two around 1000, for example `2^9 = 512` and `2^10 = 1024` (1)
Gives a maximum of `10` item inspections/comparisons (1)', '[{"criterion":"Uses a valid halving or powers-of-two method.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Gives the correct maximum number of inspections.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '`2^9 = 512` and `2^10 = 1024`, so after at most 10 inspections the search space of 1000 items has been reduced enough. The maximum is 10 comparisons.', '{"values":["10"],"numeric":true}'::jsonb, 'Award the method mark for repeated halving that reaches 1 item in 10 steps. Accept ''10 comparisons'' or ''10 inspections''.', 'Each binary search iteration approximately halves the remaining search interval. Since 1000 is less than 1024, which is `2^10`, at most 10 middle-item inspections are required.'),
  ('f185a250-ab4d-51a3-b76f-d195df79204c', '86eef3a3-02dd-5cfd-aa7a-43ecf7f4496c', 1, true, 'Outer loop performs sufficient passes, such as `FOR Pass ← 1 TO N - 1` (1)
Inner loop compares adjacent positions within the unsorted part, such as `FOR Pos ← 1 TO N - Pass` (1)
Correctly compares adjacent elements using `Mark[Pos] > Mark[Pos + 1]` for ascending order (1)
Uses a temporary variable to store one value during a swap (1)
Completes the swap by assigning both array elements correctly (1)
Uses correct structured pseudocode with matching `IF...ENDIF` and `FOR...NEXT` constructs and no out-of-range array access (1)', '[{"criterion":"Outer loop performs sufficient passes.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Inner loop compares adjacent items within valid bounds.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses the correct ascending-order comparison.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses a temporary variable during swapping.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Completes all swap assignments correctly.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses valid Cambridge-style structured pseudocode without invalid indexing.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'FOR Pass ← 1 TO N - 1
    FOR Pos ← 1 TO N - Pass
        IF Mark[Pos] > Mark[Pos + 1] THEN
            Temp ← Mark[Pos]
            Mark[Pos] ← Mark[Pos + 1]
            Mark[Pos + 1] ← Temp
        ENDIF
    NEXT Pos
NEXT Pass', '{}'::jsonb, 'Accept equivalent bubble sort variants, including a swapped flag, if they correctly sort ascending and use valid array bounds. Do not require optimisation with a flag.', 'Bubble sort repeatedly compares adjacent values and swaps them if they are out of order. After each full pass, the largest remaining value has bubbled to the end of the unsorted section.'),
  ('9348f45f-6a0d-58ae-ab15-db0cc5385c51', '75c9c96e-19e6-50ad-b4d7-b92c35f1e817', 1, true, 'Loop condition should allow a one-item interval, for example `WHILE Low <= High AND Found = FALSE` (1)
Middle should be calculated using integer division, for example `Middle ← (Low + High) DIV 2` (1)
When `Item[Middle] < Wanted`, update `Low ← Middle + 1` (1)
When `Item[Middle] > Wanted`, update `High ← Middle - 1` (1)', '[{"criterion":"Corrects the loop condition to include equality.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Corrects middle calculation to use integer division.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Corrects the update when the middle value is too small.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Corrects the update when the middle value is too large.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The loop should be `WHILE Low <= High AND Found = FALSE` so the final single item is checked. `Middle` should be calculated with integer division: `Middle ← (Low + High) DIV 2`. If `Item[Middle] < Wanted`, the target must be in the upper half, so `Low ← Middle + 1`. Otherwise the target is in the lower half, so `High ← Middle - 1`.', '{}'::jsonb, 'Award one mark per correctly identified error with a matching correction. Accept `NOT Found` for the found part of the loop condition. Do not award a mark for vague comments such as ''the halves are wrong'' unless the corrected assignments are given.', 'Binary search must continue while there is still a valid interval to search. The direction of each boundary update depends on whether the middle value is less than or greater than the target.'),
  ('89b70d5f-cc23-5557-84ef-bb5d8815d4de', '9ceaadf8-ab50-5e71-8999-004279272d63', 1, true, 'Outer loop starts at the second element and processes to `N` (1)
Stores the current value before shifting elements (1)
Initialises a position/index variable for the current item (1)
Uses a loop that checks both the lower bound and whether the previous item is greater than the current value (1)
Shifts larger previous items one position to the right and decrements the position (1)
Inserts the stored current value into the final position after the loop (1)', '[{"criterion":"Outer loop correctly processes items 2 to N.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Stores the current value before shifting.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Initialises a position variable correctly.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses correct loop conditions for lower bound and alphabetic comparison.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Shifts larger items right and decrements the position.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Inserts the stored value into the correct final position.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'FOR Index ← 2 TO N
    CurrentName ← Name[Index]
    Position ← Index
    WHILE Position > 1 AND Name[Position - 1] > CurrentName
        Name[Position] ← Name[Position - 1]
        Position ← Position - 1
    ENDWHILE
    Name[Position] ← CurrentName
NEXT Index', '{}'::jsonb, 'Accept equivalent variable names and string comparisons. Award comparison mark only if it sorts into ascending alphabetic order. Do not credit an answer that uses bubble sort instead of insertion sort for more than incidental marks.', 'Insertion sort assumes the first part of the array is already sorted. Each next name is stored, larger earlier names are shifted right, and the stored name is inserted where it belongs.'),
  ('3abbcf33-52dd-5f8d-830c-e71a6f3d1956', '10bcb707-97ec-5702-89a1-75ee74f9fdca', 1, true, 'First output row: `1, 8, 4, 19` (1)
Second output row: `5, 8, 6, 31` (1)
Third output row: `5, 5, 5, 27` (1)', '[{"criterion":"Correct first output row.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correct second output row.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correct third output row.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '| Low | High | Middle | Number[Middle] |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | 8 | 4 | 19 |
| 5 | 8 | 6 | 31 |
| 5 | 5 | 5 | 27 |', '{"rows":[["1","8","4","19"],["5","8","6","31"],["5","5","5","27"]]}'::jsonb, '', 'Middle = (1 + 8) DIV 2 = 4 and Number[4] = 19 < 27, so Low becomes 5. Middle = (5 + 8) DIV 2 = 6 and Number[6] = 31 > 27, so High becomes 5. Middle = (5 + 5) DIV 2 = 5 and Number[5] = 27, so Found becomes TRUE and the loop stops.'),
  ('2950c57e-2842-5664-b451-d5585cff8c6f', 'bcec0c2e-aa27-5d3e-b34a-4f23913454a4', 1, true, 'States or comments that insertion sort is suitable because the data is small or nearly sorted (1)
Outer loop processes from the second item to `N` (1)
Stores the current score in a temporary/current variable (1)
Initialises a position variable to the current index (1)
Uses a loop with `Position > 1` as a boundary condition (1)
Uses a comparison with the previous score, such as `Score[Position - 1] > CurrentScore`, for ascending order (1)
Shifts larger previous scores one position to the right and decrements position (1)
Inserts the stored current score into `Score[Position]` after shifting (1)', '[{"criterion":"Gives a relevant reason for choosing insertion sort.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses an outer loop from item 2 to item N.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Stores the current score before shifting.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Initialises a position variable correctly.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Checks the lower bound in the inner loop.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses the correct ascending comparison with the previous score.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Shifts larger values right and decrements the position.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Inserts the stored score at the correct position.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '// Insertion sort is suitable because the list is nearly sorted, so few shifts may be needed.
FOR Index ← 2 TO N
    CurrentScore ← Score[Index]
    Position ← Index
    WHILE Position > 1 AND Score[Position - 1] > CurrentScore
        Score[Position] ← Score[Position - 1]
        Position ← Position - 1
    ENDWHILE
    Score[Position] ← CurrentScore
NEXT Index', '{}'::jsonb, 'Award algorithm marks even if the explanatory comment appears before or after the pseudocode. Accept equivalent variable names. Do not credit a bubble sort solution as an insertion sort algorithm, but it may receive the suitability mark only if the comment about insertion sort is correct.', 'Insertion sort is often a good choice for nearly sorted data because few elements need to be shifted. The algorithm maintains a sorted section and inserts each new item into its correct position.'),
  ('c6efba1f-426c-55be-addc-0d43404963b5', '03650bdd-0525-509c-a73a-8b461d0ead1a', 1, true, 'Initialises `Low ← 1`, `High ← N`, and a suitable found flag or found position (1)
Uses a loop that continues while `Low <= High` and the item has not been found (1)
Calculates `Middle ← (Low + High) DIV 2` inside the loop (1)
Correctly handles a match between `ProductCode[Middle]` and `WantedCode` (1)
Correctly updates `Low ← Middle + 1` when the middle code is less than the wanted code (1)
Correctly updates `High ← Middle - 1` when the middle code is greater than the wanted code, and outputs either `Stock[Middle]` if found or `"Not found"` otherwise (1)', '[{"criterion":"Initialises search boundaries and found state.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses a valid binary search loop condition.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Calculates the middle position using `DIV`.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly detects a matching product code.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly updates the lower boundary when the middle value is too small.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly updates the upper boundary and produces the required final output.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Low ← 1
High ← N
Found ← FALSE

WHILE Low <= High AND Found = FALSE
    Middle ← (Low + High) DIV 2
    IF ProductCode[Middle] = WantedCode THEN
        Found ← TRUE
    ELSE
        IF ProductCode[Middle] < WantedCode THEN
            Low ← Middle + 1
        ELSE
            High ← Middle - 1
        ENDIF
    ENDIF
ENDWHILE

IF Found = TRUE THEN
    OUTPUT Stock[Middle]
ELSE
    OUTPUT "Not found"
ENDIF', '{}'::jsonb, 'Accept storing `FoundPosition ← Middle` on a match and using it after the loop. The final mark requires both correct upper-bound handling and output logic; if one is missing, consider awarding this mark only if the other five marks are fully correct and the omission is minor.', 'The product codes are sorted, so binary search can repeatedly halve the remaining possible positions. The index found in `ProductCode` is also used to access the corresponding value in `Stock`.')
on conflict do nothing;

insert into public.mark_schemes (id, question_id, version, is_current, mark_scheme, marking_points, model_answer, accepted_answers, ai_grading_instructions, explanation) values
  ('d46bc25f-e90f-5a4b-b8c2-979147e4f07c', '518fe76d-8b73-529c-b043-f79d4fbbb76e', 1, true, 'States that the titles must be sorted before binary search can be used (1)
Describes a valid studied sorting method, either bubble sort or insertion sort (1)
For bubble sort: describes adjacent comparisons and swaps; for insertion sort: describes storing current item and shifting larger previous items (1)
States that the sorting continues until the array is in ascending alphabetic order, using sufficient passes/items (1)
Initialises binary search boundaries, for example `Low ← 1` and `High ← N` (1)
Calculates the middle position using integer division, for example `(Low + High) DIV 2` (1)
Compares the requested title with `Title[Middle]` and recognises when it has been found (1)
Updates the correct boundary to discard half of the remaining array, or reports not found when the interval is empty (1)', '[{"criterion":"Explains the need to sort before binary search.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Names or describes bubble sort or insertion sort.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Gives a key mechanism of the chosen sorting algorithm.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Explains that the sorting algorithm continues until titles are in ascending order.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Initialises binary search lower and upper boundaries.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Calculates the middle index using integer division.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Compares the wanted title with the middle title and handles a match.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly explains boundary updates or not-found termination.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The titles must be sorted before binary search is used. One method is insertion sort: for each index from 2 to N, store the current title, compare it with titles in the sorted section to its left, shift larger titles one position to the right, and insert the stored title into the gap. This is repeated until `Title[1:N]` is in ascending alphabetic order.

To search, set `Low ← 1` and `High ← N`. While `Low <= High` and the title has not been found, calculate `Middle ← (Low + High) DIV 2`. If `Title[Middle]` is the requested title, output that it has been found. If the requested title is alphabetically after `Title[Middle]`, set `Low ← Middle + 1`; otherwise set `High ← Middle - 1`. If the loop ends without a match, output `Not found`.', '{}'::jsonb, 'Accept either bubble sort or insertion sort for the sorting section. Award credit for clear explanation; full executable pseudocode is not required. Do not award binary search boundary marks if the answer describes linear search only.', 'The scenario combines the two main requirements: sorting data into a known order and then applying binary search. Binary search is efficient for repeated searches, but only after the titles are sorted.'),
  ('4875a811-169d-5169-89db-ad3b9e532d51', '9f725f25-f563-56a3-aa29-659dff12eddd', 1, true, 'Adjacent / neighbouring elements are compared and swapped if they are in the wrong order (the first is greater than the second for ascending order) (1)', '[{"criterion":"Compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'It compares each pair of adjacent elements and swaps them if the first is larger than the second.', '{"values":["compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order"]}'::jsonb, 'Both ideas are needed: adjacent/neighbouring elements AND swap when out of order. Accept ''if Times[j] > Times[j+1] swap''.', null),
